stt=1.00 sim=0.820 f0=218/43 aprovadas=1/1 | In the 1290s she wrote, in Old French, the language of the people,
stt=1.00 sim=0.811 f0=217/28 aprovadas=1/1 | and not in Latin, the language of the clergy,
stt=1.00 sim=0.860 f0=206/32 aprovadas=1/1 | a book called The Mirror of Simple Annihilated Souls,
stt=1.00 sim=0.841 f0=197/26 aprovadas=1/1 | who remain only in will and desire of Love.
stt=1.00 sim=0.842 f0=202/36 aprovadas=1/1 | It is a dialogue between characters: the Soul, Love and Reason.
stt=1.00 sim=0.879 f0=191/29 aprovadas=1/1 | The Soul passes through seven stages until it lets go of everything that is only its own:
stt=1.00 sim=0.795 f0=213/38 aprovadas=1/1 | self-will, pride, even fear.
stt=1.00 sim=0.820 f0=212/31 aprovadas=1/1 | Then it becomes transparent, like a mirror, to divine love.
stt=1.00 sim=0.785 f0=216/36 aprovadas=1/1 | Marguerite calls this annihilation.
stt=1.00 sim=0.873 f0=211/28 aprovadas=1/1 | It is not the death of the person; it is the end of the ego's prison.
stt=1.00 sim=0.854 f0=195/32 aprovadas=1/1 | Such a soul, she wrote, no longer lives out of fear of punishment or by bargaining with heaven.
stt=1.00 sim=0.776 f0=196/31 aprovadas=1/1 | It lives free, because it loves.
stt=1.00 sim=0.866 f0=185/41 aprovadas=1/1 | For the powers of that time, this freedom was the danger:
stt=1.00 sim=0.859 f0=200/34 aprovadas=1/1 | a soul that finds God through love no longer depends on intermediaries.